WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.757
A good crust is worthless if nobody can afford it. So

2
00:00:02.857 --> 00:00:05.290
we build the cost from the ground up, and ask a

3
00:00:05.390 --> 00:00:08.363
blunt question. Is it cheaper than what people use today?

4
00:00:09.133 --> 00:00:12.054
Two prongs, one bill. Fermentation for the polymer,

5
00:00:12.154 --> 00:00:14.719
feedstock and enzyme for the cementing, and a

6
00:00:14.819 --> 00:00:17.444
single fermentation setup shared between them.

7
00:00:18.333 --> 00:00:22.180
Now plot cost per hectare against the area you treat. Chemical spray

8
00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:25.315
is a flat rate, you pay it on every hectare. Our crust

9
00:00:25.415 --> 00:00:28.798
carries an upfront setup, so on a tiny plot it is expensive,

10
00:00:28.898 --> 00:00:31.817
but that cost spreads out as the treated area grows.

11
00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:36.722
Past a break-even of a few tens of hectares,

12
00:00:36.822 --> 00:00:39.817
the biological crust is simply the cheaper option, and

13
00:00:39.917 --> 00:00:42.167
it keeps getting cheaper on larger sites.

14
00:00:42.266 --> 00:00:45.595
Concrete matting sits far above the top of this chart. It is a

15
00:00:45.695 --> 00:00:49.245
permanent surface, not a treatment, and it is here only for scale.

16
00:00:49.333 --> 00:00:52.878
And an honest note on carbon. The cementing does lock away carbon

17
00:00:52.978 --> 00:00:56.355
dioxide, but the credit is worth only a few dollars a hectare,

18
00:00:56.455 --> 00:00:59.833
against a cost over a thousand. So the captured tonnage is the

19
00:00:59.933 --> 00:01:02.973
result to report, not something that pays for the crust.

